JThaiPDF
==========================================================================
The Thai language PDF generating utilities for Java
==========================================================================

Targets JasperReports 7.x (net.sf.jasperreports.pdf.JRPdfExporter).

Two ways to get correct Thai rendering:

1. JasperReports built-in glyph renderer (recommended for modern TrueType
   fonts; no jthaipdf code needed). Add this property at report or context
   level -- Thai is NOT enabled by default:

       <property name="net.sf.jasperreports.export.pdf.glyph.renderer.blocks.x"
                 value="thai"/>

   Then export with a stock JRPdfExporter and an embedded Thai TTF (e.g.
   Sarabun). Optionally also set
   net.sf.jasperreports.export.pdf.glyph.renderer.add.actual.text=true so
   copied text keeps its logical character order.

2. jthaipdf's glyph rearrangement (use when your embedded font carries the
   legacy Thai Private Use Area glyphs, 0xF7xx -- many common Thai fonts do,
   e.g. TH Sarabun New):

       - replace JRPdfExporter with ThaiJRPdfExporter, or
       - use ThaiExporterManager to export to a file or stream

   ThaiDisplayUtils.toDisplayString(String) is public and standalone if you
   need to apply the rearrangement yourself.

Pick whichever renders the stacking correctly with the fonts you actually
ship. Option 1 works with any Thai TTF; option 2 needs a font that includes
the legacy PUA glyphs (many do). PdfRenderHarnessTest (under src/test) renders
both ways to target/thai-native.pdf and target/thai-library.pdf for comparison.

NOTE (2.0.0-jr7): upgraded from JasperReports 4.0.1 to 7.x. The exporter API
changed (getChunk now returns net.sf.jasperreports.pdf.common.PdfTextChunk and
receives the chunk text as a String; JRExporterParameter was removed). The old
iText helper ThaiChunk (com.lowagie.text.Chunk subclass) has been removed --
the modern PDF layer is abstracted behind a producer, so direct-iText usage is
no longer provided; call ThaiDisplayUtils directly if you need it.


